    "content": "./docs/manual/options.md:1062:The Partitioning element specifies Cyclone DDS network partitions and how DCPS partition/topic combinations are mapped onto the network partitions.\n./docs/manual/options.md:1092:The NetworkPartitions element specifies the Cyclone DDS network partitions.\n./docs/manual/options.md:1100:This element defines a Cyclone DDS network partition.\n./docs/manual/options.md:1108:This attribute specifies the addresses associated with the network partition as a comma-separated list. The addresses are typically multicast addresses. Non-multicast addresses are allowed, provided the \"Interface\" attribute is not used: * An address matching the address or the \"external address\" (see General/ExternalNetworkAddress; default is the actual address) of a configured interface results in adding the corresponding \"external\" address to the set of advertised unicast addresses.\n./docs/manual/options.md:1110:Readers matching this network partition (cf. Partitioning/PartitionMappings) will advertise all addresses listed to the matching writers via the discovery protocol and will join the specified multicast groups. The writers will select the most suitable address from the addresses advertised by the readers.\n./docs/manual/options.md:1128:This attribute specifies the name of this Cyclone DDS network partition. Two network partitions cannot have the same name. Partition mappings (cf. Partitioning/PartitionMappings) refer to network partitions using these names.\n./docs/manual/options.md:1136:The PartitionMappings element specifies the mapping from DCPS partition/topic combinations to Cyclone DDS network partitions.\n./docs/manual/options.md:1144:This element defines a mapping from a DCPS partition/topic combination to a Cyclone DDS network partition. This allows partitioning data flows by using special multicast addresses for part of the data and possibly also encrypting the data flow.\n./docs/manual/options.md:1152:This attribute specifies a partition and a topic expression, separated by a single '.', that are used to determine if a given partition and topic maps to the Cyclone DDS network partition named by the NetworkPartition attribute in this PartitionMapping element. The expressions may use the usual wildcards '\\*' and '?'. Cyclone DDS will consider a wildcard DCPS partition to match an expression if there exists a string that satisfies both expressions.\n./docs/manual/options.md:1160:This attribute specifies which Cyclone DDS network partition is to be used for DCPS partition/topic combinations matching the DCPSPartitionTopic attribute within this PartitionMapping element.\n./docs/manual/config/cyclonedds_specifics.rst:253:The ``Partitioning`` element in the configuration allows configuring ``NetworkPartition`` elements and mapping topic/partition names to these “network partitions” using ``PartitionMapping`` elements.\n./docs/manual/config/cyclonedds_specifics.rst:257:Unicast and multicast addresses in a network partition play different roles:\n./docs/manual/config/cyclonedds_specifics.rst:265:The mapping of a data reader or writer to a network partition is indirect: first the partition and topic are matched against a table of *partition mappings*, partition/topic combinations to obtain the name of a network partition, then the network partition name is used to find the addressing information. This makes it easier to map many different partition/topic combinations to the same multicast address without having to specify the actual multicast address many times over. If no match is found, the default addresses are used.\n./docs/manual/config/cyclonedds_specifics.rst:269:A single reader or writer is associated with a set of partitions, and each partition/topic combination can potentially map to a different network partition. In this case, the first matching network partition will be used. This does not affect what data the reader will receive; it only affects the addressing on the network.\n./docs/manual/config/cyclonedds_specifics.rst:505:Although the SEDP protocol never requires any configuration, network partitioning does\n./src/CMakeLists.txt:28:option(ENABLE_NETWORK_PARTITIONS \"Enable network partition support\" ON)\n./src/ddsrt/include/dds/features.h.in:24:/* Whether or not support for network partitions is included */\n./src/core/ddsc/tests/nwpart.c:202:    { .name = \"p0\", .address_string = \"192.168.1.1\", .interface_names = \"eth1\" },  // both unicast & interface\n./src/core/ddsc/tests/nwpart.c:203:    { .name = \"p1\", .address_string = \"\", .interface_names = \"not_an_interface\" }, // non-existent interface\n./src/core/ddsc/tests/nwpart.c:204:    { .name = \"p2\", .address_string = \"239.255.0.8\", .interface_names = \"nomc\" },  // multicast address on non-mc intf\n./src/core/ddsc/tests/nwpart.c:205:    { .name = \"p3\", .address_string = \"239.255.0.8,193.168.0.1\", .interface_names = \"\" }, // multicast address on non-mc intf\n./src/core/ddsc/tests/nwpart.c:206:    { .name = \"p4\", .address_string = \"239.255.0.8\", .interface_names = \"\" },      // multicast address, no mc-capable intf\n./src/core/ddsc/tests/nwpart.c:207:    { .name = \"p5\", .address_string = \"7.7.7.7\", .interface_names = \"\" },          // no matching interface\n./src/core/ddsc/tests/nwpart.c:208:    { .name = \"p6\", .address_string = \"192.168.1.7\", .interface_names = \"\" },      // network part of address, but host part non-zero\n./src/core/ddsc/tests/nwpart.c:210:    { .name = \"p7\", .address_string = \"192.168.1.1\", .interface_names = \"\" },\n./src/core/ddsc/tests/nwpart.c:211:    { .name = \"p8\", .address_string = \"\", .interface_names = \"lo,eth0\" },\n./src/core/ddsc/tests/nwpart.c:212:    { .name = \"p9\", .address_string = \"239.255.0.8\", .interface_names = \"eth1,lo,eth0\" },\n./src/core/ddsc/tests/nwpart.c:213:    { .name = \"pA\", .address_string = \"192.169.1.1\", .interface_names = \"\" },     // matching should also work on external address\n